ne of AMD's a series ap  use which combines a CPU and GPU onto a  single chip but they're pricey and  performance isn't as good as when using  a dedicated GPU instead I went for AMD's  FX  300 fish Recife you based on the  Piledriver architecture it still can't  quite keep up with the best I 5s and I  seventh of the world but it's close  and it'll smoke the costlier dual core  i3 s thanks to his decent clock speed  and six cores for the GPU I went with  AMD's older but still excellent and  brilliantly overclockable Radeon HD 7850  in fact the recently released r7 265 is  just a rebranded version of the card  with a slight clock speed bump and with  retailers clearing out stock to make way  for the 265 you can pick up a 7850 for  just over a hundred bucks from retailers  like Newegg i overthought this one to  1050 megahertz and memories of 1450  megahertz  with little effort putting the GPU  squarely in 7873 territory with the two  most important components selected the  rest are easy to pick out based on the  amount of cash I had left for the  motherboard I went with Asus is casually  named m5 a 7/8 L - M / USB 3 a.m. 3 +  which is based on the 760 G chipset in a  micro ATX form factor  it lacks niceties such as PCI Express  3.0 and SATA 6 but it does feature USB 3  as well as a sis's famed motherboard  reliability making it great value at  around $50 power comes courtesy of an 80  plus rated EVGA 500 watt PSU at $45 some  might consider that overkill but it  gives us Headroom for future upgrades  and overclocking and you never skimp on  the PSU unless you like the thought of  potentially frying your shiny new  components storage is handled by 500  gigabyte Seagate Drive at around 50  bucks which is a fast 7200 rpm model and  that should give us a relatively good  amount of storage with games Ram comes  in the form of a stick of Kingston  HyperX XMP blue series running at 1600  megahertz budget constraints me like it  only stretched to 4 gigabytes at around  40 bucks but if you've got a few extra  bucks to plow into your PC here's where  I suggest you spend it and pick up eight  gigabytes to house the components I went  with factors call one hundred case it's  a case I've used before on  PC builds and at less than $40 is one of  the cheapest micro ATX cases out there  or at least one of the cheapest that  doesn't look completely terrible and  isn't a total pain to work inside plus  it's got some nice airflow features with  120 millimeter fan in the front space  for a 92 millimeter in the back a  removable side mounted hard disk tray  and the option to add another hundred 20  mil fan to the side of the case the  total cost just a smidge over the target  at five hundred and sixty six dollars  and sixteen cents was that extra 16  bucks worth it well let's put this  sucker together and find out  [Music]  so here she is the finished article now  there's not a lot of cable management  options in the core 1000 but cheap  doesn't have to mean untidy by using one  of the five and a half inch drive bays  to hide the cables we can still get  something that's relatively clean  and that gives us good airflow but  enough of the fluff let's get Windows on  this bad boy and benchmark us some games  mech base shooter titanfall runs and a  heavily modified version of valves  Source engine which isn't the most  demanding of game engines despite that  the Xbox one famously only runs the  gamer 792p whereas the AMD rig has no  trouble running it at its higher  settings at 1080p and with two times  multi sampling anti-aliasing on and a  locked at 60 frames per second  battlefield 4 is a much more graphically  demanding shooter on ps4 it runs at 1600  by 900 whereas the Xbox one runs it at  720p using the high settings preset at  1080p with anti-aliasing off the AMD rig  pushes around 72 frames per second on  average and still looks the business  cranking it to ultra with two times  multi sampling anti aliasing we see a  noticeable drop in framerate at 1080p to  around 42 frames per second which makes  it still very playable if you're willing  to drop down to the ps4 1600 by 900  resolution you'll get some very slick  visuals and an easily locked 60 frames  per second Assassin's Creed 4 is another  demanding title one that the Xbox one  only pushes at 900 P while the ps4  manages a full and deliciously sweet  1080p both run at only 30 frames per  second though at high settings at 1080p  with FX AAA on the AMD Rig boasts a  frame rate of around 47 frames per  second interestingly bumping up to very  high with FXAA didn't massively affect  the frame rate dropping it down slightly  to 42 frames per second a lock 30 frames  per second at the highest settings is  possible then giving you a better  looking experience than on the next-gen  consoles  based on Unreal Engine three thief was  never going to present too much of an  issue quite many other games the Xbox  one version is only rendered at 900p  while the ps4 runs at 1080p with  everything turned on to the higher  settings with the exception of super  sampling anti-aliasing which remained  off the AMD Rig pushed 87 frames per  second on average making a lock 60  absolutely no trouble at all  so there you have it folks is it  possible to build a gaming PC on a  next-gen budget absolutely there are  some compromises to be made along the  way and if you can stretch to an extra  $100 or so you'll have a PC that I'll  keep you gaming for some time more  Ramsay obvious choice it doesn't cost  much to bump it up to 8 gigabytes and  the less time the PT has to spend  thrashing the hard drive for a swap file  the better an extra $70 towards an r2 17  GPU would be a wise decision too it's  good value and overclocks extremely well  putting it firmly in the high end GPU  segment for just a fraction of the cost  there's also the option of an SSD for a  more responsive feel and aftermarket  cooler for CPU overclocking and a nicer  looking case but they're not essential  the fact is though instead of buying an  Xbox one or even a ps4 you can build  yourself a great gaming PC that will run  games even better than those consoles do  currently and you'll have access to a  vast cheaper library of games via  services like Steam or you'll find  triple-a releases indie gems and free to  play games like dota 2 that you simply  can't get on a console and hey if you  really want to you can hook up a  controller plug it into your TV and kick  back on the sofa in your living room too  that's it from me but be sure to check  out Pete's video to see how he got on  with his Intel and NVIDIA based PC and  check out the full article over on  GameSpot see you next time  you
 

